\subsection{Implemented SpectralMSE schedule}
\label{sec:riccati-similarity-design-guidance}
\label{sec:riccati-deployed-cadence}
For the target cadence
\begin{equation}
T_{S,\star}=\clip(c_T\tau_\star,T_{S,\min},T_{S,\max}),
\label{eq:engineering-pseudo-cadence}
\end{equation}
the implemented regularizer target is
\begin{equation}
\boxed{r_{S,\star}=C_Jq_{\mathrm{eff}}^{1/14}
\widehat\sigma_{a,B,\star}^{\,6/7}
\tau_\star^{24/7}T_{S,\star}^{-1/2}.}
\label{eq:adapt-default-rs-law}
\end{equation}
The configured value is $C_J=0.0538$.
\label{eq:adapt-mse-coefficient}
Its interpretation follows from \eqref{eq:adapt-mminus4-similarity}; the
current record-wise evaluation does not uniquely derive this fixed value.
It is a design setting, whereas the diagnostic coefficients in
Table~\ref{tab:adapt-exact-mse-exponents} vary with spectrum. The code divides
$c_\sigma$ out of $\sigma_{aw,\star}$ to recover the physical statistic,
keeping OU-prior amplitude and regularization independently configurable.

The cadence dependence is already included: no second cadence factor is
applied. Away from cadence clamps, $T_S\propto\tau$ reduces the period exponent
to $24/7-1/2=41/14$; the acceleration exponent is $6/7$.
\label{eq:adapt-mse-unclipped-scaling}\label{eq:adapt-reduced-mse-law}
For dimensional comparison only,
\begin{align}
r_{S,\rm dim}&=C_R\sigma_{aw}\tau^3,
\label{eq:adapt-rs-base}\\
(\omega_R\tau)^6&=\frac{q_{\rm eff}}{C_R^2\sigma_{aw}^2T_S}.
\label{eq:adapt-cR-spectral-meaning}
\end{align}
This coordinate scale is not an adaptation law for the deployed filter.
